\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 To create a Kubernetes Pod configuration that includes  containers (such as the ems_ui, ems_identity, and ems_employee containers), we need to define a Pod YAML file. To run multiple containers within a single Pod  and need to work together (e.g., sharing the same network, storage, or logs).\par
\par
Step 1: Define the Pod YAML File for Your Containers\par
\cf1 vim pod.yaml\par
\par
\cf2 apiVersion: v1\par
kind: Pod\par
metadata:\par
  name: ems-app-pod\par
spec:\par
  containers:\par
    - name: ems-ui\par
      image: lakshmipriyapbt/ems-employee:u1\par
      ports:\par
        - containerPort: 7000\par
      resources:\par
        limits:\par
          memory: "512Mi"\par
          cpu: "500m"\par
        requests:\par
          memory: "256Mi"\par
          cpu: "250m"\par
    - name: ems-identity\par
      image: lakshmipriyapbt/ems-employee:i1\par
      ports:\par
        - containerPort: 9000\par
      resources:\par
        limits:\par
          memory: "512Mi"\par
          cpu: "500m"\par
        requests:\par
          memory: "256Mi"\par
          cpu: "250m"\par
    - name: ems-employee\par
      image: lakshmipriyapbt/ems-employee:e1\par
      ports:\par
        - containerPort: 8000\par
      resources:\par
        limits:\par
          memory: "512Mi"\par
          cpu: "500m"\par
        requests:\par
          memory: "256Mi"\par
          cpu: "250m"\par
\par
\cf1 Explanation of the YAML file:\cf2\par
\cf3 apiVersion: Specifies the version of the Kubernetes API to use. For Pods, it's v1.\par
kind: Indicates that the object being created is a Pod.\par
metadata: Defines the metadata of the Pod, such as its name. In this case, the Pod will be named ems-app-pod.\par
spec: The specification for the Pod itself.\par
containers: A list of containers that will run inside the Pod. In your case, three containers are defined.\par
name: The name of the container inside the Pod (e.g., ems-ui, ems-identity, ems-employee).\par
image: The Docker image that each container will use (e.g., lakshmipriyapbt/ems-employee:u1 for the ems-ui container).\par
ports: A list of container ports to expose. For example, the ems-ui container exposes port 7000, ems-identity exposes port 9000, and ems-employee exposes port 8000.\par
resources: You can define CPU and memory limits and requests.\par
requests: The amount of CPU and memory Kubernetes should reserve for the container.\par
limits: The maximum amount of resources the container is allowed to use\par
\par
\cf0\par
Step 2: Apply the Pod YAML File\par
Once you've created the pod.yaml file, you can apply it using the following kubectl command:\par
\cf1 kubectl apply -f pod.yaml\par
\par
\cf0 Step 3: Verify the Pod\par
To check if the Pod has been created and is running\par
\cf1 kubectl get pods\par
\cf0\par

