Step 5: Expose the Pod (Optional)\par
 to expose your Pod to the outside world (e.g., for accessing the web UI from a browser or making your services accessible), you can create a Service.\par
expose the Pod with a NodePort to make it accessible on a specific port on the Kubernetes nodes:\par
\cf1 kubectl expose pod ems-app-pod --port=80 --target-port=7000 --name=ems-ui-service --type=NodePort\par
kubectl expose pod ems-app-pod --port=9000 --target-port=9000 --name=ems-identity-service --type=NodePort\par
kubectl expose pod ems-app-pod --port=8000 --target-port=8000 --name=ems-employee-service --type=NodePort\par
\cf0\par
Each of these commands exposes the containers' ports via Kubernetes Services. You can get the exposed NodePort by running:\par
\cf1 kubectl get services\par
\cf0\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Step 6: Clean Up--emove the Pod and all its containers from the cluster.\par
\cf1 kubectl delete pod ems-app-pod\cf0\par
