\cf3\fs32 Scaling: If you need to scale the containers (run multiple replicas), you might want to use a Deployment instead of a Pod. A Deployment allows Kubernetes to manage the number of replicas of your Pods and ensures high availability\cf0\fs22 .\par
\par
\par
\par
************************************************\par
*******************************************************\par
1....scale your Pod using a Deployment instead of a single Pod\par
scale your app to 3 replicas (in a Deployment):\par
vim \par
apiVersion: apps/v1\par
kind: Deployment\par
metadata:\par
  name: ems-app-deployment\par
spec:\par
  replicas: 3\par
  selector:\par
    matchLabels:\par
      app: ems-app\par
  template:\par
    metadata:\par
      labels:\par
        app: ems-app\par
    spec:\par
      containers:\par
        - name: ems-ui\par
          image: lakshmipriyapbt/ems-employee:u1\par
          ports:\par
            - containerPort: 7000\par
        - name: ems-identity\par
          image: lakshmipriyapbt/ems-employee:i1\par
          ports:\par
            - containerPort: 9000\par
        - name: ems-employee\par
          image: lakshmipriyapbt/ems-employee:e1\par
          ports:\par
            - containerPort: 8000\par
\par
Apply the Deployment:\par
\cf1 kubectl apply -f ems-app-deployment.yaml\cf0\par
\par
2.....\par
Expose the Pod (or Deployment): If you want to expose your Pod to the outside world or to other services in the cluster, you can create a Service.\par
\cf1 kubectl expose pod ems-app-pod --port=80 --target-port=7000 --name=ems-ui-service --type=NodePort\par
\cf0\par
\cf3\fs32 Monitor and Auto-Scale:\cf0\par
\fs22 You can set up Horizontal Pod Autoscaling to scale your Pods automatically based on CPU or memory usage.\par
Use Kubernetes Monitoring Tools \cf3 like Prometheus and Grafana\cf0  to track your app\rquote s performance and resource usage over time.\par
\par
\par
\b\par
\fs32 Stopping Kubernetes Pods:\fs22\par
If you want to stop the Kubernetes pods, you can delete the pod or scale down the deployment to 0 replicas.\par
\par
To delete the pod:\par
\cf1 kubectl delete pod ems-app-pod\par
\cf0\par
This will delete the pod, and Kubernetes will eventually recreate it (depending on your deployment settings).\par
\par
Alternatively, if the pod is part of a deployment, you can scale down the deployment:\par
\cf1 kubectl scale deployment ems-app-deployment --replicas=0\par
\cf0\par
This will scale the deployment to zero replicas, effectively stopping the pods.\par
\par
\cf3\par
\ul\fs36 Key Differences: Kubernetes vs Docker Swarm\par
\ulnone\fs22 Kubernetes Pods are isolated units of deployment for containers that can run on one or more nodes in a cluster. Kubernetes manages container orchestration, scaling, and healing automatically.\par
Docker Swarm is a container orchestration tool built into Docker. It handles clustering, service discovery, and load balancing for containerized applications.\par
Depending on your environment, you can choose to stop one or both of these orchestration platforms. If you\rquote re moving towards Kubernetes, you might want to stop your Docker Swarm setup to avoid conflicts or resource issues.\par
\par
\cf1\ul\fs32 ERROR\cf3\ulnone\fs22\par
\cf1 Here getting issue that 3 containers are running but 1 container is getting down after few seconds and t restarting.\par
\cf4  Insufficient Memory or CPU Resources\par
If your UI container exceeds the requested resources or encounters memory limits, Kubernetes will terminate the container and attempt to restart it.\par
This means:\par
\par
Requests: The amount of memory and CPU Kubernetes will guarantee for the container.\par
Limits: The maximum memory and CPU the container can consume before Kubernetes may kill and restart it.\par
If your application is using more than 512Mi memory or 500m CPU, Kubernetes will kill the container. To resolve this, you can:\par
\par
Increase the memory and CPU limits for the UI container if the application is resource-intensive.\par
Monitor your resource usage to confirm whether your container is being killed due to resource exhaustion.\par
How to increase resource limits:\par
You can increase the memory and CPU limits to see if it resolves the issue\par
\cf3\par
\cf1 resources:\par
  limits:\par
    memory: "1Gi"  # Increase memory to 1GB\par
    cpu: "1000m"   # Increase CPU limit to 1 core\par
  requests:\par
    memory: "512Mi"\par
    cpu: "500m"\par
\par
\cf3 Apply the changes with:\cf1\par
kubectl apply -f pod.yaml\par
